do you know what happens when you buy  stock most people would assume that  their brokerage would go out and get a  stock certificate under their name this  way even if the brokers collapsed those  shares would belong to you but this is  actually not what happens  when you buy a stock from your brokerage  you basically get an iou contract from  the brokerage this contract gives you  certain rights to the stock but not  ownership before you get mad at the  brokerages though you should know that  they don't own the stock either when  they want to buy or sell a stock on  behalf of their clients they have to  send their order to the depository trust  company or the dtc also known as seed co  the dtc will issue the brokerage and iou  statement and this is what gets passed  down to you  so it's actually the dtc that not only  owns your stock but also virtually all  publicly traded shares and securities in  the us and 131 other countries according  to their official website their job is  to bring efficiency to the securities  industry by retaining custody of wait  for it  54.2 trillion dollars  and that was as of july 2017 since then  the u.s stock market has grown 18  trillion dollars and the us has issued  trillions of dollars worth of bonds so  the dtc's total assets are likely well  above 70 trillion dollars today so  here's how seed and co grew to owning  the world  taking a look back the story of dtc  dates back to the 1960s  america had not only recovered from the  great depression but the economy and  more specifically the automotive  industry was booming americans were  making and spending more money than ever  before most of these guys had no  interest in saving or investing either  they themselves or their parents had  lost everything to the great depression  so they felt that it was way better to  just live in the moment this is what  really spawned the terrible but  extremely common trend of living  paycheck to paycheck if your retirement  can be ruined by some irresponsible  financial institution why save in the  first place despite this popular  sentiment given how large and diverse  america is there were naturally some  people who still wanted to invest their  money and given that these guys were  making more money than ever before they  were able to invest more money than ever  before  this resulted in unprecedented trade  volume leading into the 1970s the new  york stock exchange was regularly  processing more than 8 million shares  per day  now compared to today 6 billion shares  per day this was nothing but they didn't  have computers or advanced technology  that could just automatically execute  each trade so they had to manually  handle 8 million shares every single day  and this was basically their limit  so in 1968 the niazi decided to create a  securities clearing firm called the  central certificate service or the ccs  the ccs was basically responsible for  automating the stock market the  president of the nazi at the time robert  hack promised quote we are going to  automate the stock certificate out of  business by substituting a punch card  instead of keeping track of ownership  through stock certificates the ccs kept  all the stock certificates to themselves  and tracked ownership through electronic  records the nyz hoped to reduce the  prevalence of stock certificates by 75  with the ccs but there was one major  issue two-thirds of brokers refused to  use the service  electronic records were extremely  attractive for brokers but they didn't  trust the ccs or computers in general  for that matter so most brokers opted to  continue in their old ways  despite this the ccs continued to expand  their services and attempted to build  credibility in 1970 for instance cscs's  services were extended to the american  stock exchange  around the same time we also saw the  creation of the banking and securities  industry committee or basic  basic give bankers and brokerages a way  to propose rules and standards that the  ccs must follow banks and brokers  deliberated for years about how ccs  should be run but eventually in 1973  basic members agreed to create a beefed  up version of the ccs called the dtc  once a dtc was created more and more  brokerages jumped on board though most  brokerages didn't use the ccs they  wanted to use it they simply didn't want  to explain to their clients that they  will no longer receive stock  certificates because the brokerage is  either too lazy or didn't want to handle  that much paper but as notable banks and  brokerages embraced the dtc smaller  institutions flooded in now that's all  cool and all but how exactly does the  dtc work  well i explained it as an iou contract  at the beginning but it's of course not  that simple whenever a brokerage or bank  agrees to work with the dtc the dtc will  take all of the institutions stock  certificates bond certificates and money  market instruments and enter them into  their database the dtc already owns  billions if not trillions of dollars  worth of each security so they'll mark  down that chase has rights to 10.4  percent of dtc's apple shares and 12 of  their google shares and so on and so  forth as buy and sell orders flow in  during the day the dtc tags up the total  inflow and outflow of each of their  clients for each security the dtc simply  changes the numbers in their database to  reflect the result of the trading day  for example chase's stake in apple might  be reduced to 10.2 percent and their  stake in google might be increased to  12.2 percent but no stock certificates  are actually being swapped and no paper  is being traded the dtc only settles  securities at the end of each day and  each security takes different amount of  times to process based on their time  sensitivity for example options have  expiration dates so they're processed  within one business day stocks on the  other hand take two business days while  slow moving assets like bonds take three  days if you have a cash brokerage  account you're probably well aware of  these settlement times once you sell a  stock you actually have to wait two days  to get your money but with most  brokerage accounts the brokerage will  simply give you the money instantly and  they'll be reimbursed by the dtc over  the next few days so a lot of stock  buyers are completely unaware of  settlement times but that's what's going  on in the background whenever you buy or  sell  buying and selling only accounts for a  small portion of the stock market though  most people simply hold on to their  stocks for years if not decades  so what happens to shares that are  simply held  well the dtc gives their clients a  certain amount of rights to their shares  for example clients will retain voting  rights the right to use the shares as  collateral and a right to buy and sell  options using the underlying stock the  dtc will also process dividend payments  and ensure that each client is paid out  appropriately in essence the dtc  basically takes care of everything i  think it's pretty clear why banks prefer  this over trading billions of stock  certificates per day  all of this still leaves the question is  your brokerage or bank associated with  the dtc  well if you live in the u.s the answer  is yes unless you keep your money in  some obscure offshore bank it's almost a  certainty that your brokerage and bank  work directly with the dtc the dtc does  publicly post all the institutions they  work with so you can go check out that  list if you're interested but the  easiest way to identify affiliation with  the dtc is to simply check if your  brokerage allows for transferring to or  from other brokerages brokerages don't  want to complete transfers manually  especially for customers that are  leaving so if you have the ability to  easily transfer to another brokerage  it's because the brokerage simply  offloads a transferring responsibility  to the dtc also just because your  brokerage is associated with the dtc  doesn't mean that it's impossible for  you to get a stock certificate it is  nearly impossible as brokers just put up  as many roadblocks as they can and i  really don't recommend you try to do  this but if you want the certificate for  novelty purposes or something you can  ask your broker and they're required by  state law to give you your certificate  the process will likely take months  you'll probably be charged hundreds of  dollars and you'll end up spending hours  on the phone with your brokerage but if  you're super persistent you should be  able to get it  this tool is changing fast though it's  just a matter of time until it's  impossible to get a stock certificate in  the uk for example stock certificates  will no longer be a thing starting in  2025. this is really nothing to worry  about as it's simply the far overdue  digitalization of this part of the  industry but as you would guess this has  sparked a lot of conspiracies regarding  the dtc  before we put on our tinfoil hats let's  discuss some legitimate concerns with  seed co  first of all given the sheer volume of  money seed handles it's possible that an  insider steals some of that money  stealing one billion dollars from seed  would be like stealing one dollar from  someone who has 54 thousand dollars  stealing 10 million dollars would be  like stealing just one cent seed only  has 12 directors and half dozen  employees  so theoretically it wouldn't be that  hard for insiders to collude and steal  serious amounts of money  again this is improbable but seeing  cases like bernie madoff it is possible  the second more serious concern is  illegal short selling the process is  super convoluted and technical but in  essence hedge funds were able to  leverage the dtc settlement time and a  borrowing program to engage in naked  short selling which is illegal the  problem can probably be addressed by  some changes in regulation but the  government is too lazy to do anything  about it until it actually leads to some  sort of tangible consequences so in the  meantime the dtc is enabling naked short  selling and finally the third major  concern is that there's very little  transparency from the dtc  given the scale of money that they deal  with all of their activities should be  public knowledge and heavily scrutinized  but only the dtc and their banker  clients have access to dtc's daily  activities making this information  public wouldn't necessarily prevent the  wallet out scheme from taking place but  it would reduce the chances  anyway moving on to the conspiracy some  people believe that seed co is owned by  the billionaires of the world and used  to crash the stock market for their  benefit seed co is a private company and  it's officially owned by its clients but  some believe that these clients are  actually just a handful of powerful  families like the rockefellers and the  rothschilds there seems to be some sort  of unverifiable evidence that these  families have staked in the company but  even if they did it doesn't have to be  nefarious if you were rich you'd  probably deal with the ddc directly as  well instead of going through brokers  another piece of evidence that's used to  propel this conspiracy is that in the  early 1990s jim mcneff who was a  director at the dtc told the media that  the dtc's first control test was four or  five years ago this means that he was  probably referring to black monday and  the stock market crash of 1987. trading  volume is generally the highest during  stock market crashes because investors  are most emotional during this time  periods in 2007 for instance the dtc  sell transactions that were worth 513  trillion dollars so i'm pretty sure jim  was just referring to testing if the dtc  could officially handle that much volume  but conspiracies believe that jim  slipped up and accidentally admitted to  causing the crash  now is the dtc intertwined with a bunch  of hedge funds and market maker  manipulation games i'm sure they are  but do i believe that the dtc is a  conspiracy organization created  specifically to crash and pump markets  no not really  and even if that is the case it's not  like we can do anything about it  so it's probably best to not look too  into it but that's just what i think do  you guys think seed co is involved in  nefarious activities comment that down  below  also drop a like if you've never heard  about this company until now and of  course consider joining our discord  community to suggest future video ideas  and consider subscribing to see more  questions logically answered but until  then i'm hari and i'll see you guys on  the next one

